# Run artifacts are large, machine-specific and reproducible from the tools. 701 MB of them, and
# 846 JSON files. They are evidence for a verdict, not source: drop the contents.
*
!.gitignore

# EXCEPT the two kinds that are neither large nor reproducible.
#
# 1. The written reports. A verdict cites `reports/<piece>-survey.md` as its evidence, and until
#    now that path was unversioned — so an agent that overwrote one had destroyed the thing a
#    verdict rests on with no history to recover it from. That happened: the W1-05 builder
#    overwrote its own journey artifact, restored what it could exactly, and had to flag two
#    fields it could not recover rather than invent them. 106 files, 800 KB.
# 2. The blog feed. It is the owner's view of progress and it is append-only by convention, which
#    is exactly the file a careless `>` destroys.
# git cannot re-include a file inside an excluded DIRECTORY, so descend first, then pick.
!*/
!**/*.md
!blog-feed.jsonl

# 3. BASELINES. Corrected by the W1-MASS builder, which caught the reasoning above being wrong for
#    exactly one class of file: "that test is right for a measurement and exactly wrong for a
#    baseline, whose whole value is being the OLD value." A run artifact can be regenerated
#    because you want today's number. A baseline exists to be yesterday's, so regenerating it
#    destroys the only thing it was for. The render-probe baseline had already rotted — all three
#    of its pixel-exact hashes differ from HEAD, changed eight hours earlier by another piece —
#    with nothing going red, because no gate re-runs it and git was not holding the old value.
!**/*baseline*.json
!**/*-fingerprint*.json

# 4. THE SABOTAGE REPLAY CORPUS, by the same argument, pushed one step further. The nine
#    artifacts `tools/experience/sabotage.mjs --cases` replays are not merely files you would
#    rather not regenerate — SEVERAL OF THEM CANNOT BE REGENERATED AT ALL. §0_goes_red of the
#    W1-04 collision report is the PRE-FIX `__w1_04_townSolids`; the fix landed and that verb
#    does not exist at HEAD. Two of the souls artifacts are browser runs with guards deleted from
#    `sim/souls.js` and `engine.js`; both guards are present at HEAD. The test above — "is this
#    reproducible by re-running the tool that made it" — answers NO, so by the file's own rule
#    they belong in history. Excluding them meant a fresh clone reported ABSENT and exited 8
#    (W1-25-r1 §C): honest, and useless.
#    They live as byte-for-byte tracked twins under reports/experience/sabotage-corpus/, written
#    and hash-verified by `tools/experience/sabotage-corpus.mjs`. 379 KB, nine files, plus a
#    SHA-256 manifest so a twin that drifts from an origin still on the container is caught.
!**/sabotage-case-*.json

# 5. PINS. `*` above is the right default and it had one consequence nobody reconciled: a verdict
#    that cited `reports/**.json` was citing something a fresh clone did not have, so 56 of 76
#    Wave-1 verdicts failed a real checkout while passing on the container that made their
#    evidence. The fix is per-file, not a blanket exception — `node tools/verdict-evidence.mjs
#    --recover` force-adds the small cited files by name, so scratch stays excluded and evidence
#    does not. Above 1 MiB it writes a PIN instead: `<path>.pin.json`, the decisive numbers plus a
#    SHA-256 of the raw bytes plus the command that regenerates them. 108.9 MiB of the 119.3 MiB
#    of missing evidence was three raw frame traces; the pins are 3 KiB and carry more (a
#    committed trace can be silently regenerated, a pinned one cannot — `--verify` goes red).
#    Pins are tracked by rule, not by force-add, so a future one cannot be lost the same way.
!**/*.pin.json

# Still ignored, deliberately: reports/runs/ raw logs (tools/run.mjs writes there by design),
# every .json artifact, and every capture. Those are reproducible by re-running the tool that
# made them, which is the test for whether something belongs in history.
reports/runs/

# 7. `land-rescue/` IS THE SAFETY NET AND IT STAYS UN-IGNORED. Read this before "tidying" it.
#
#    `tools/land.mjs` writes a pre-resolution copy of any file a merge would otherwise discard to
#    `reports/land-rescue/<utc>/<path>` — its own comment calls that "the difference between
#    resolved and discarded". So these are not run artifacts and rule 1's reproducibility test does
#    not apply: **nothing re-runs to produce them.** Checked 2026-08-15 on two singletons whose live
#    counterparts still exist — `w1-30-shadow-casters.mjs` and `docs/data/roadmap.json` — and the
#    rescued bytes appear NOWHERE in the history of their live paths. The mechanism has already saved
#    content that was otherwise gone.
#
#    On 2026-08-15 the accumulated snapshots were REMOVED FROM THE WORKING TREE and left in history
#    (see `git show 128eab4a:reports/land-rescue/...`). That is a working-tree cleanup, not a
#    deletion, and it was done for one measured reason: 291 files, 68 MB, holding **88 stale copies
#    of `orchestration/INDEX.md` against 1 live one** and 198 stale `progress.html`/`index.html`/
#    `status.json` copies. An agent grepping the index hit 89 candidates and one was current — the
#    same shape as the reference item that told builders for eight days that no images were vendored.
#
#    **Do NOT add an ignore rule here.** Ignoring the directory would leave future rescues untracked
#    and therefore genuinely losable — which is the failure the mechanism exists to prevent. Let them
#    accumulate, and sweep them out of the working tree again when they get in the way; history keeps
#    every byte, and `git log --diff-filter=D -- reports/land-rescue` finds the sweep.

# 6. A SEALED BLIND KEY IS NOT EVIDENCE, IT IS THE ANSWER SHEET — and rule 2's `!**/*.md` was
#    quietly letting parts of one into the repo. Noticed 2026-08-15: `PAIRING-KEY.json` is
#    correctly caught by `*`, but the five `pair0N.reveal/make-pair.PROMPT.md` files under
#    `protocol-a-w1-r2-SEALED-KEY/` were re-included by the markdown rule and sat untracked,
#    one `git add -A` away from the branch.
#
#    Why that matters more than tidiness: a blind judge in this project CANNOT be made naive —
#    the pack-builder established that a subagent inherits the project CLAUDE.md — so the only
#    thing standing between a judge and the answer is that the answer is not in the repo. A
#    directory literally named `.reveal`, tracked and greppable, removes that. It would not have
#    voided round 2 (decoded and recorded in `orchestration/status/I3-PROTOCOL-A-R2-RESULT.json`),
#    but ring 0's `I3` has 96 unrun gates ahead of it and this pattern would follow every one.
#
#    So: nothing inside a sealed-key directory is ever tracked, whatever its extension. This sits
#    AFTER every re-include on purpose — a later rule wins in gitignore, which is exactly the
#    ordering that let the .md rule reach in here. One-sided by construction: it can only ever
#    exclude, so it cannot cause anything to be lost that a re-run of the pack builder would not
#    reproduce.
**/*SEALED-KEY*/
